tion\n- bundle_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `52`\n- resolved_references: `17`\n- anchored_references: `35`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `3`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `4`\n- summary: The numbers match the evaluator outputs and the grounded claims. Four wording issues remain: ungrounded BenchBench and redundancy statements, an ungrounded missingness claim with a wrong scale description, an unsupported sealed-run identity claim, and a hyperparameter table whose source is not tied to the sealed run.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `benchbench-redundancy-ungrounded` (Abstract and Related Work (Benchmark suites and their overlap)): Only titles and metadata of the cited papers were retained, with no abstracts or snippets. The grounded claim says only that agreement analyses \"bear on how related benchmark scores are\". The specific BenchBench finding (reference set, correlation measure, strong dependence) and the \"strongly redundant\" premise have no support. The previous audit round already asked for this level of detail to be removed, and it is still in the Related Work paragraph and the abstract.\n  - claim: Related Work: \"Perlitz2024BenchBench show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly\". Abstract: \"historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant\".\n  - evidence: references_provenance.json entry Perlitz2024BenchBench (title and venue only); claims_grounding.json claim \"Agreement analyses across benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models bear on how related benchmark scores are\"; paper.tex Related Work paragraph 1 and Abstract sentence 1. The Introduction already uses the softer \"plausibly overlapping\".\n  - requested_fix: Reduce the BenchBench sentence to \"agreement analyses across benchmarks \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench} bear on how related benchmark scores are\", and drop the \"therefore\" motivation clause. Change the abstract to \"benchmarks may be substantially redundant\" or similar hedged wording.\n- `scope-missingness-scales-unsupported` (Discussion and Limitations (Scope, Threats to validity 3, Practical reading)): No missingness analysis against model strength or recency is in claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json or analysis/*.json, so these are stated as facts without evidence. The scale list is also wrong. The HELM mean-win-rate column is excluded, and the retained HELM columns are F1, EM, equivalent-CoT and BLEU-4. The Open LLM Leaderboard scores are raw 0-1 values. The Practical reading adds \"well-covered\" selection, which the evidence does not test, and the measured k grids are 1-20 depending on the leaderboard.\n  - claim: \"Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks)\"; \"Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency\"; \"scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1)\"; \"a handful of well-covered benchmarks ... already remove a large part of the column-mean error\"\n  - evidence: source_evidence/manifests/helm_final_manifest.json (score_definition: \"Mean win rate column excluded\"; the columns are F1, EM, Equivalent (CoT), BLEU-4); analysis/protocol_facts.json (no missingness statistics); claims_grounding.json has no missingness claim; paper.tex Discussion \"Scope\" and \"Threats to validity\" (3).\n  - requested_fix: Reword the missingness points as a possible concern (\"if missingness correlates with model strength, the missing-at-random assumption may fail; we did not test this\"). Replace the scale list with \"accuracy-type, F1, exact-match and BLEU-type metrics\". Remove \"well-covered\" from the Practical reading, or label it as an untested conjecture.\n- `sealed-identity-either-or-ungrounded` (Abstract, Method (Compared approaches), Results (configuration comparison), Conclusion): The evidence trail records only the final program_tree_sha256 (sha256:3f76de56...). It carries no hash for any search configuration, so it cannot be compared with the retained source copies. It also does not show that the sealed program was one of exactly two configurations. The \"either/or\" restriction is an inference from \"best-scoring on development\", and nothing in the artifacts records the selection. The hash does not match the simple pair either. The sealed tree could be a different or modified tree. Grounding claim 8 cites only final.evidence.episodes and final.qualification.outcome, which do not support the restriction or the \"matches neither\" statement.\n  - claim: \"the sealed run used one member of this family (the described pair or its latent-skill extension)\"; \"The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension\"; \"the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy\"\n  - evidence: paper_data.json: all five final-evaluation entries carry the same program_tree_sha256, and the top_programs and all_programs entries carry none. claims_grounding.json claim 8 source_ids (final.evidence.episodes, final.qualification.outcome). The Limitations section states that \"no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used\". There is no hash-comparison artifact in source_evidence/.\n  - requested_fix: State that the identity of the sealed-evaluated source tree is not established. Say that the configuration was presumably the development-selected pair or its extension, but this is not recorded. Remove \"matches neither retained source copy\" unless a comparison artifact is cited, and apply the same hedged wording in the abstract and conclusion.\n- `si-hyperparameters-not-sealed-config` (Supplementary Information (Method specification and hyperparameters) and Method (Selector and imputer)): The constants and the 70/150/210 s guards come from the proposed-pair source copy, which the paper says is not verified as the sealed tree. The Method text presents the guards and the degradation behavior as properties of the evaluated method without that caveat. The program source also shows that the 150 s guard switches to a quick tuning grid rather than the Gaussian-only model. Only the 210 s stacking guard falls back to the Gaussian-only model.\n  - claim: \"Cost and applicability ... the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds\"; \"Several steps are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model\"\n  - evidence: source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py lines 60-62 and 733-738 (FULL_TUNE_BUDGET leads to QUICK_SHRINKS, QUICK_RANKS and QUICK_LAMS; STACK_BUDGET leads to the Gaussian-only model); paper.tex Method \"Cost and applicability\" versus the SI Table hyper caption.\n  - requested_fix: Qualify the Cost and applicability sentences as properties of the proposed-pair source copy. Describe the guards accurately: optional candidates are skipped after 70 s, tuning falls back to a smaller grid after 150 s, and stacking is skipped (Gaussian-only) after 210 s.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well supported overall: the methods (EM, stacking, NNLS, submodular selection, ridge), item-level cheaper-evaluation work, and latent-capability work are all cited, and the quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. Three targeted gaps remain. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **related-work-comparators** (Related Work, high, comparator)\n   - Anchor: \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices\"; \"here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\".\n   - The closest prior work predicts unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, applies low-rank or matrix-completion methods to LLM score matrices, or selects what to run adaptively. The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources and item-level work. The paper's differentiation claim is therefore not positioned against its direct competitors.\n   - Search topics: predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a benchmark subset; matrix completion or low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark matrices; adaptive or IRT-based active selection for LLM evaluation.\n\n2. **intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim** (Introduction, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"are known to overlap in what they measure\".\n   - The premise that motivates budgeted evaluation is cited only to the suites themselves, which do not document redundancy.\n   - Search topics: correlation or redundancy among LLM benchmarks; low-dimensional structure in benchmark scores.\n\n3. **data-open-llm-leaderboard-context** (Method, protocol data paragraph, medium, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models\".\n   - The leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets, and its six component benchmarks are named without citations. HELM and MTEB are cited.\n   - Search topics: Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; IFEval, BBH, GPQA, MuSR and MMLU-Pro papers.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\n- Adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\n- Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\n- Empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Rank-Gauss transform and kNN: standard components, and the draft already states them as such.\n- Numeric results and protocol facts: grounded in evidence files.\n- Limitations and discussion text: deliberately self-critical, with no external claims that need support.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
