{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UTmQC25uWymW7FFnuA2diq","type":"tool_result","content":"=== 36\nEvaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a family of selector--imputer pairs built from a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers; the sealed run used one member of this family (the described pair or its latent-skill extension, which the available evidence cannot tell apart). Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The evaluated pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n=== 66\n\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n=== 95\n\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition as the best-scoring configuration. The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension (below); the two have identical development scores, the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy, and the available evidence cannot say which ran. Statements about the ``evaluated pair'' therefore refer to this selector--imputer family. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\n=== 98\nThe constants and routines below are read from the proposed-pair source copy; the sealed run used either this pair or its latent-skill extension (Section~\\ref{sec:variants}).\n=== 106\n\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{dataset},k)$ episode is dominated by EM and cross-validation; the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds per seed. The method presumes a history matrix with enough coverage per benchmark and a new model whose scores are exchangeable with history models after transformation, which family-disjoint evaluation tests but does not guarantee. Several steps are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model.\n=== 201\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n=== 205\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\emph{Configuration identity and freezing.} The configuration was chosen on the development partition before the sealed evaluation, but no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used or that it was run only once; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same design could behave differently.\n=== 207\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and a residual error of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains for the evaluated pair on MTEB at $k{=}20$, with no claim about a lower bound.\n=== 244\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the proposed-pair source copy (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). This copy is not verified to be the exact tree evaluated in the sealed run (see below), so the constants describe the proposed pair, not a confirmed record of the sealed configuration. % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\n=== 271\nThe longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The main text describes the shared stack, and the extension is described here because the sealed run may have used it. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this proble\n50:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its sc\n70:\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public \n95:\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition as the best-scoring configuration. The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or \n98:The constants and routines below are read from the proposed-pair source copy; the sealed run used either this pair or its latent-skill extension (Section~\\ref{sec:variants}).\n106:\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{dataset},k)$ episode is dominated by EM and cross-validation; the sealed evaluation of\n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (si\n111:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$