{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ma6SgfByrRajx7L5Pfxdyy","type":"tool_result","content":"\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly; the 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge is small and unresolved. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has a mean relative error 1.6\\% lower than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\nBoth figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the averaged skills suggest, but cannot confirm because per-baseline errors were not retained, that the greedy-forward ridge baseline was no better than the random-$k$ ridge baseline. This indicative inference is the only retained evidence about selection alone, and it does not establish that a leave-one-out greedy selector is worse than random subsets under a fixed ridge imputer. For H3 the grounded conclusion is therefore modest. The pair's accuracy over a ridge imputer does not separate into a selector effect and an imputer effect, and the small margin over random-$k$ suggests that on these leaderboards most of the achievable gain over column means is available with a reasonable imputer and an arbitrary subset.\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and permuting the observations raises nRMSE by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes, so the predictions use the new model's own scores. The retained evidence does not report control skill against column means. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked selector--imputer family (one member of it, not identifiable from the retained evidence) on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the averaged skills only suggest, without per-baseline errors to confirm, that a leave-one-out greedy ridge baseline was no better than random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that b\n95:\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition a\n106:\\paragraph{Cost and applicability.} All components are CPU-only NumPy. Fitting plus selection for one $(\\text{datase\n205:\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation\n210:We posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unse","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb371e10-197a-4f15-ac67-4967a43d2078","uuid":"5bd4dc78-d9e0-4720-97ae-4f7c2ca774cb","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:16:01.260Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly; the 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge is small and unresolved. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has a mean relative error 1.6\\% lower than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\nBoth figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the averaged skills suggest, but cannot confirm because per-baseline errors were not retained, that the greedy-forward ridge baseline was no better than the random-$k$ ridge baseline. This indicative inference is the only 